ICENSE】  | 【94†LICENSE】  |    |\nL205: 【95†LICENSE-APACHE】  | 【95†LICENSE-APACHE】  |    |\nL206: 【96†LICENSE-MIT】  | 【96†LICENSE-MIT】  |    |\nL207: 【97†NOTICE】  | 【97†NOTICE】  |    |\nL208: 【98†README.md】  | 【98†README.md】  |    |\nL209: 【99†rust-toolchain.toml】  | 【99†rust-toolchain.toml】  |    |\nL210: [Button: View all files]\nL211: ## Repository files navigation\nL212: \nL213:   *   * 【100†README】\nL214:   * 【100†License】\nL215:   * 【100†Apache-2.0 license】\nL216:   * 【100†MIT license】\nL217: \nL218: More items\nL219: # MegaZK (open-source)\nL220: \nL221: GPU-accelerated Plonky2 proving.\nL222: This repository is the open-source implementation of MegaZK, the system described in the ICS '26 paper 【101†MegaZK: A Memory Efficient GPU System Accelerating End-to-end Zero-Knowledge Proof†dl.acm.org】 (DOI: 【102†10.1145/3797905.3807842†doi.org】). Citation: Muyang Li, Yueteng Yu, Bangyan Wang, Xiong Fan, Mingyu Gao, and Shuwen Deng. 2026. In Proceedings of the 40th ACM International Conference on Supercomputing (ICS '26). ACM, 1165–1176. 【102†https://doi.org/10.1145/3797905.3807842†doi.org】\nL223: The GPU proving path lives in the forked 【103†`plonky2`】 crate and calls into 【104†`era-boojum-cuda`】 / 【105†`era-cuda`】 for CUDA kernels and runtime bindings.\nL224: \nL225: The default Cargo feature set is CPU-only and does not build CUDA dependencies. In the benchmark programs, `--baseline` selects the CPU prover; the GPU path is opt-in with `--features gpu`. Running a non-baseline benchmark without that feature returns `GPU proving is unavailable; rebuild with --features gpu`.\nL226: This monorepo is dual-licensed MIT OR Apache-2.0. See 【94†`LICENSE`】 and 【97†`NOTICE`】.\nL227: ## Repository Layout\nL228:   * 【103†`plonky2`】: forked Plonky2 workspace. GPU proving enters through `build_gpu` / `GpuCircuitData::prove` and the `plonky2/plonky2/src/integration` module.\nL229:   * 【104†`era-boojum-cuda`】: CUDA kernels and Rust wrappers used by the GPU prover.\nL230:   * 【105†`era-cuda`】: CUDA runtime bindings.\nL231:   * 【106†`plonky2-sha256`】, 【107†`plonky2-sha512`】, 【108†`plonky2-examples`】, 【109†`proto-neural-zkp`】: benchmark applications.\nL232:   * 【110†`plonky2_ed25519`】: experimental Ed25519 benchmark. Proof generation is expected to run, but verification currently has a known issue (`BENCHMARK_VERIFY_STATUS: KNOWN_ISSUE`). That is not a regression.\nL233:   * 【111†`plonky2-gpu-bench`】: std-only internal helper for shared benchmark timing, verification policy, and CUDA memory reporting.\nL234: ## Current Reproduction Environment\nL235: \nL236: The following environment is the current known reproduction baseline. Other versions may work, but have not been normalized for this open-source snapshot.\nL237:   * OS: Ubuntu 22.04.5 LTS, Linux 6.8.0-124-generic, x86_64\nL238:   * Rust: `nightly-2023-11-28` (pinned in 【99†`rust-toolchain.toml`】)\nL239:     * `rustc 1.76.0-nightly (49b3924bd 2023-11-27)`\nL240:     * `cargo 1.76.0-nightly (9b13310ca 2023-11-24)`\nL241:   * CUDA toolkit: 12.x (this snapshot was built with 12.5, `nvcc V12.5.82`)\nL242:   * CMake: 3.30.3\nL243:   * clang: 14.0.0\nL244:   * GPU/driver: requires an NVIDIA GPU and driver compatible with CUDA 12.x. If CMake cannot detect the GPU architecture during build, set `CUDAARCHS` manually, for example `CUDAARCHS=89`.\nL245: The repository has a pinned toolchain in 【99†`rust-toolchain.toml`】. For CPU baseline comparisons, `RUSTFLAGS=-Ctarget-cpu=native` can improve performance on the local machine.\nL246: ## Support Matrix\nL247: \nL248: Benchmark  | GPU proof  | CPU baseline  | Verification status\nL249: --- | --- | --- | ---\nL250: Factorial  | Supported  | Supported  | Expected to pass\nL251: Fibonacci  | Supported  | Supported  | Expected to pass\nL252: SHA256  | Supported  | Supported  | Expected to pass\nL253: SHA512  | Supported  | Supported  | Expected to pass\nL254: ECDSA  | Supported  | Supported  | Expected to pass\nL255: MVM  | Supported  | Supported  | Expected to pass\nL256: Ed25519  | Experimental  | Experimental  | Known verification issue\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nInternal Error ()\nciteturn1view2 [wordlim: 200] Source: open({\"ref_id\":\"https://developer.nvidia.com/blog/employing-cuda-graphs-in-a-dynamic-environment/\",\"lineno\":null}); \nFailed to fetch https://developer.nvidia.com/blog/employing-cuda-graphs-in-a-dynamic-environment/: (400) Timeout fetching--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\n (https://people.cs.georgetown.edu/jthaler/TExtendedAbstractPub.pdf)\nciteturn1view3 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: today; Content type: application/pdf; Number of pages: 17; Source: open({\"ref_id\":\"https://people.cs.georgetown.edu/jthaler/TExtendedAbstractPub.pdf\",\"lineno\":null}); Total lines: 1037\nL0@P0: Time-Optimal Interactive Proofs for Circuit Evaluation\nL1@P0: Justin Thaler ∗\nL2@P0: Abstract\nL3@P0: Several research teams have recently been working toward the development of practical general\u0002purpose protocols for verifiable computation. These protocols enable a computationally weak verifier to\nL4@P0: offload computations to a powerful but untrusted prover, while providing the verifier with a guarantee\nL5@P0: that the prover performed the requested computations correctly. Despite substantial progress, existing\nL6@P0: implementations require further improvements before they become practical for most settings. The main\nL7@P0: bottleneck is typically the extra effort required by the prover to return an answer with a guarantee of\nL8@P0: correctness, compared to returning an answer with no guarantee.\nL9@P0: We describe a refinement of a powerful interactive proof protocol due to Goldwasser, Kalai, and\nL10@P0: Rothblum [20]. Cormode, Mitzenmacher, and Thaler [14] show how to implement the prover in this\nL11@P0: protocol in time O(SlogS), where S is the size of an arithmetic circuit computing the function of interest.\nL12@P0: Our refinements apply to circuits with sufficiently “regular” wiring patterns; for these circuits, we bring\nL13@P0: the runtime of the prover down to O(S). That is, our prover can evaluate the circuit with a guarantee\nL14@P0: of correctness, with only a constant-factor blowup in work compared to evaluating the circuit with no\nL15@P0: guarantee.\nL16@P0: We argue that our refinements capture a large class of circuits, and we complement our theoretical re\u0002sults with experiments on problems such as matrix multiplication and determining the number of distinct\nL17@P0: elements in a data stream. Experimentally, our refinements yield a 200x speedup for the prover over the\nL18@P0: implementation of Cormode et al., and our prover is less than 10x slower than a C++ program that simply\nL19@P0: evaluates the circuit. Along the way, we describe a special-purpose protocol for matrix multiplication\nL20@P0: that is of interest in its own right.\nL21@P0: Our final contribution is the design of an interactive proof protocol targeted at general data parallel\nL22@P0: computation. Compared to prior work, this protocol can more efficiently verify complicated computa\u0002tions as long as that computation is applied independently to many different pieces of data.\nL23@P0: 1 Introduction\nL24@P0: Protocols for verifiable computation enable a computationally weak verifier V to offload computations to a\nL25@P0: powerful but untrusted prover P. These protocols aim to provide the verifier with a guarantee that the prover\nL26@P0: performed the requested computations correctly, without requiring V to perform the computations herself.\nL27@P0: Surprisingly powerful protocols for verifiable computation were discovered within the computer science\nL28@P0: theory community several decades ago, in the form of interactive proofs (IPs) and their brethren, interactive\nL29@P0: arguments (IAs) and probabilistically checkable proofs (PCPs). In these protocols, the prover P solves a\nL30@P0: problem using her (possibly vast) computational resources, and tells V the answer. P and V then engage in\nL31@P0: a randomized protocol involving the exchange of one or more messages. During this exchange, P’s goal is\nL32@P0: to convince V that the answer is correct.\nL33@P0: ∗Harvard University, School of Engineering and Applied Sciences. Supported by an NSF Graduate Research Fellowship and\nL34@P0: NSF grants CNS-1011840 and CCF-0915922.\nL35@P0-1: 1Results quantifying the power of IPs, IAs, and PCPs are some of the most celebrated in all of com\u0002putational complexity theory, but until recently they were mainly of theoretical interest, far too inefficient\nL36@P1: for actual deployment. In fact, the main applications of these results have traditionally been in negative\nL37@P1: applications – showing that many problems are just as hard to approximate as they are to solve exactly.\nL38@P1: However, the surging popularity of cloud computing has brought renewed interest in positive appli\u0002cations of protocols for verifiable computation. A typical motivating scenario is as follows. A business\nL39@P1: processes billions or trillions of transactions a day. The volume is sufficiently high that the business cannot\nL40@P1: or will not store and process the transactions on its own. Instead, it offloads the processing to a commercial\nL41@P1: cloud computing service. The offloading of any computation raises issues of trust: the business may be con\u0002cerned about relatively benign events like dropped transactions, buggy algorithms, or uncorrected hardware\nL42@P1: faults, or the business may be more paranoid and fear that the cloud operator is deliberately deceptive or has\nL43@P1: been externally compromised. Either way, each time the business poses a query to the cloud, the business\nL44@P1: may demand that the cloud also provide a guarantee that the returned answer is correct.\nL45@P1: This is precisely what protocols for verifiable computation accomplish, with the cloud acting as the\nL46@P1: prover in the protocol, and the business acting as the verifier. In this paper, we describe a refinement of an\nL47@P1: existing general-purpose protocol originally due to Goldwasser, Kalai, and Rothblum [14, 20]. When they\nL48@P1: are applicable, our techniques achieve asymptotically optimal runtime for the prover, and we demonstrate\nL49@P1: that they yield protocols that are significantly closer to practicality than prior work.\nL50@P1: We also make progress toward addressing another issue of existing interactive proof implementations:\nL51@P1: their applicability. The protocol of Goldwasser et al. (henceforth the GKR protocol) applies in principle to\nL52@P1: any problem computed by a small-depth arithmetic circuit, but this is not the case when more fine-grained\nL53@P1: considerations of prover and verifier efficiency are taken into account. In brief, existing implementations\nL54@P1: of interactive proof protocols for circuit evaluation require that the circuit have a highly regular wiring\nL55@P1: pattern [14,37]. If this is not the case, then these implementations require the verifier to perform an expensive\nL56@P1: (though data-independent) preprocessing phase to pull out information about the wiring of the circuit, and\nL57@P1: they require a substantial factor blowup (logarithmic in the circuit size) in runtime for the prover relative\nL58@P1: to evaluating the circuit without a guarantee of correctness. Developing a protocol that avoids these pitfalls\nL59@P1: and applies to more general computations remains an important open question.\nL60@P1: Our approach is the following. We do not have a magic bullet for dealing with irregular wiring patterns:\nL61@P1: if we want to avoid an expensive pre-processing phase for the verifier and minimize the blowup in runtime\nL62@P1: for the prover, we do need to make an assumption about the structure of the circuit we are verifying. Ac\u0002knowledging this, we ask whether there is some general structure in real-world computations that we can\nL63@P1: leverage for efficiency gains.\nL64@P1: To this end, we design a protocol that is highly efficient for data parallel computation. By data paral\u0002lel computation, we mean any setting in which one applies the same computation independently to many\nL65@P1: pieces of data. Many outsourced computations are data parallel, with Amazon Elastic MapReduce1 being\nL66@P1: one prominent example of a cloud computing service targeted specifically at data parallel computations.\nL67@P1: Crucially, we do not want to make significant assumptions on the sub-computation that is being applied, and\nL68@P1: in particular we want to handle sub-computations computed by circuits with highly irregular wiring patterns.\nL69@P1: The verifier in our protocol still has to perform an offline phase to pull out information about the wiring\nL70@P1: of the circuit, but the cost of this phase is proportional to the size of a single instance of the sub-computation,\nL71@P1: avoiding any dependence on the number of pieces of data to which the sub-computation is applied. Similarly,\nL72@P1: the blowup in runtime suffered by the prover is the same as it would be if the prover had run the basic GKR\nL73@P1: protocol on a single instance of the sub-computation.\nL74@P1: 1http://aws.amazon.com/elasticmapreduce/\nL75@P1-2: 2Our final contribution is to describe a new protocol specific to matrix multiplication that is of interest in\n"
